// @run
// @log ka
// @log ka
// @log kc
// @log ia
// @log a
// @log n
// @log i
// A NARROWED literal-union PARAMETER (`K & !"kb"`) and every consumer of it. This was
// `xfail-miscompile-narrowed-litunion-param-string-return.vl` and makes that kind's documented
// trip to `@run`.
//
// IT WAS NEVER A MISSING WIDEN. The three sibling defects it was filed with were VALUE sinks —
// an atom reaching a string slot that had no widening arm. This one is a REP DISAGREEMENT: the
// parameter is ALREADY declared `i32`, and the predicates that decide whether to treat it as an
// atom said no. `print(v)` emitted the raw id, a `: string` return lowered the id into a
// reference, and the CALLER lowered the member literal as a string into the i32 slot — three
// symptoms, one wrong question.
//
// THE QUESTION. `nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias` asks whether the arena type is a REGISTERED alias
// index. Narrowing does not preserve the index: `K & !"kb"` is a fresh copy, so the registry
// misses even though `K` is registered. `tyLitUnionAliasIx` asks the same question through the
// checker's COPY-PROVENANCE table as well, which records exactly those minted copies — and it
// is the predicate the REP LAYER already uses to assign the valtype (`emit_rep`'s `TyUnion`
// arm: an alias index reps `i32`, anything else is uncovered). Asking it makes the consumers
// agree with the rep by construction instead of by coincidence.
//
// THE BARE INLINE SPELLING IS THE CONTROL, and it is the whole reason a STRUCTURAL test is
// wrong here. A first attempt used `tyIsLitUnion`, which is true for all three spellings — but
// they do not share a rep:
//
//   v: K                 -> (param i32)      registered alias
//   v: K & !"kb"         -> (param i32)      copy-provenance
//   v: "ia" | "ib"       -> (param (ref 1))  canon-softens to `string`
//
// A structural predicate claims the atom rep for that third row too, swapping one miscompile
// for another. `pInline` and `takesInline` below pin that it stays a string reference.
//
// BOTH SIDES MOVED TOGETHER. The callee reads through `exprIsLitAtom`; the caller lowers its
// argument through `cParamLitUnion`. Fixing only the callee makes the caller's string literal
// land in the now-atom slot (`expected i32, found (ref $type)`) — the same twin rule the
// widening sinks taught, in a different pair.
type K = "ka" | "kb" | "kc"
type N = K & !"kb"

function takesN(v: N): string { v }
function takesK(v: K): string { v }
function takesInline(v: "ia" | "ib"): string { v }

function pAlias(v: K) { if v == "ka" { print("a") } }
function pNarrow(v: N) { if v == "ka" { print("n") } }
function pInline(v: "ia" | "ib") { if v == "ia" { print("i") } }

function main() {
  print(takesN("ka"))
  const n: N = "ka"
  print(n)
  print(takesK("kc"))
  print(takesInline("ia"))

  pAlias("ka")
  pNarrow("ka")
  pInline("ia")
}
main()
